 To find out how much Alan will have in his bank account after 10 years, we need to use the formula for compound interest, which is given by: \[
A = P\left(1 + \frac{r}{n}\right)^{nt}
\] where \(A\) is the amount of money accumulated after \(n\) years, including interest. \(P\) is the principal amount (the initial amount of money), \(r\) is the annual interest rate (decimal), \(n\) is the number of times that interest is compounded per year, and \(t\) is the time the money is invested for in years. In this case, \(P = 500\) dollars, \(r = 3\% = 0.03\) (since we need the interest rate as a decimal), \(n = 1\) (since the interest is compounded annually), and \(t = 10\) years. Plugging these values into the formula, we get: \[
A = 500\left(1 + \frac{0.03}{1}\right)^{1 \times 10}
\] Simplifying inside the parentheses gives: \[
A = 500\left(1.03\right)^{10}
\] Calculating \(\left(1.03\right)^{10}\) gives approximately 1.3439. Multiplying this by the principal amount, 500 dollars, gives us: \[
A \approx 500 \times 1.3439 = 671.95
\] Rounded to the nearest dollar, Alan will have approximately \$672 in his bank account after 10 years.</think> <answer>672</answer>

